1. The Goals part is saved for the first time. Workbook records that save as a change and gives the change an ID, say `c1`. The text it saved is now called "version `c1`".
2. Alice and Bob each read the Goals part. Each is told the text and that it is version `c1`.
3. Alice saves new text and says "I started from `c1`". The current version is still `c1`, so her save goes in as change `c2`. The text is now version `c2`.
4. Bob saves his text and also says "I started from `c1`". The current version is `c2`, so Workbook knows Bob never saw Alice's text. It does not replace hers.
5. Workbook compares three texts: `c1` (where both started), `c2` (Alice's) and Bob's.
   - If they changed different lines, the combined text is saved as `c3`, recorded as a merge of the two.
   - If they changed the same lines, Bob gets a conflict that shows all three texts.

- **Why a version is named by the change and not by the Git commit.** When Bob's unpublished work is replayed on top of Alice's, Git gives his commits new IDs. The change IDs stay the same. A version name that a person or agent noted earlier therefore still points at the same text.
- **Why the starting version is stored with the edit.** It is the only way to tell "Bob replaced Alice's text on purpose" from "Bob never saw it". It is also what gives the merge its starting text. It is in the record format from the first release, because it cannot be added to old edits later.
- **The check in step 4 is made twice.** [Proposed]
  - First by the command, when Bob saves.
  - Again every time any copy of Workbook works out a relic's current text from its history. A body edit counts only if the version it says it started from was the current one at that point. An edit that fails this test stays in history under its author's name, and it does not change the text.
  - The second check exists because the first can be skipped: by a bug, by another tool, or by someone writing to Git directly. Without it, such an edit would quietly replace newer text, and two clones could disagree about what the current text is. With it, every clone arrives at the same text.
